const HTML = "<p>When I was eighteen, I asked my sister whether I was ugly. I wanted reassurance, though I had presented it as a request for information, and she took the request seriously.</p>\n<p>&quot;You&#39;re not ugly. You&#39;re like your dad. Kinda funny-looking.&quot;</p>\n<p>My father was a perfectly ordinary-looking man. Nobody was likely to ask him to model anything, but there was nothing wrong with his face, and my sister hadn&#39;t said there was anything wrong with mine either. She had made a comparison I understood and supplied a qualification I could have lived without. I returned to that sentence occasionally for the next twenty-five years, though she was probably finished with it before the end of the afternoon.</p>\n<p>I can&#39;t blame her for what I did with it. I was already worried about my looks; that was why I had asked. Her answer became a piece of evidence for a belief I had brought to the conversation, and I would look in the mirror and find the same verdict waiting for me. After enough repetitions, I stopped treating it as a judgement. It was something I knew about myself.</p>\n<p>Knowing made decisions easier. It cancelled Saturday afternoons, made some plans seem pointless before I had tried them, and affected how I approached women whose attention I wanted. I could spare us both the embarrassment if I assumed in advance that they wouldn&#39;t be interested. I was making choices on behalf of women who hadn&#39;t been consulted.</p>\n<p>The absence of their interest could then pass for confirmation, a convenient arrangement for a belief that decides which evidence I will encounter and presents the result as independent support.</p>\n<p>By my late twenties, my response to my face had begun to change, though I can&#39;t give this a satisfying date or a single conversation that corrected the old one. I remember a morning in the bathroom when I realised I had stopped wincing at my reflection. Whatever had happened had been happening for a while before I noticed it.</p>\n<p>I hadn&#39;t become a different-looking man, nor had I managed to prove that I was handsome. I was simply no longer having quite the same unpleasant meeting with myself every time I saw a mirror, which made me wonder how much of what I called self-knowledge consisted of conclusions I had stopped checking.</p>\n<p>Some of those conclusions had seemed especially credible because they were unkind. Calling myself unattractive felt more honest than calling myself attractive; there is a certain prestige in refusing to flatter yourself. But an accusation doesn&#39;t become accurate because it hurts to accept it. I had given the harsher answer an advantage without asking it to earn one.</p>\n<p>Replacing it with praise didn&#39;t seem promising either. If I had stood in front of the mirror announcing how wonderful I looked, I would have known who was trying to persuade whom. The original objection would still have been there, waiting for my little speech to finish.</p>\n<p>A study by Joanne Wood and her colleagues gives a narrow example of that problem. In two experiments published in 2009, participants with low self-esteem who repeated a positive statement about being lovable, or concentrated on its truth, felt worse than the relevant comparison groups, while participants with high self-esteem benefited modestly. The result doesn&#39;t settle what builds confidence, but it makes me less willing to prescribe reassuring sentences to someone who finds them impossible to believe. <a href=\"https://www.psychologicalscience.org/journals/psychological-science/j.1467-9280.2009.02370.x/\">Wood, Perunovic and Lee, 2009</a></p>\n<p>I would rather have a more accurate acquaintance with myself. What do I enjoy when there is nobody to impress? What work do I actually return to, and who do I want to call when something good happens? The answers won&#39;t all be flattering, and some will change, but at least they concern something I do rather than a verdict I have learnt to repeat.</p>\n<p>That allows for a much less dramatic description of my failings. I can be bad at something without making it an explanation of my entire personality, or be awkward on a date without establishing that I am incapable of being wanted. A particular disappointment leaves room for another attempt. A fixed account of who I am can rule it out before it happens.</p>\n<p>I still recognise my sister&#39;s description. There is a family resemblance; she wasn&#39;t imagining it. What I no longer accept is the amount of authority I gave it: she answered a question about my face, and I used the answer to decide which plans were worth making.</p>\n";

